			<div class="content" id="post-content-3900919">
									

<p>I love this. I still think getting caught up on the self-insert &quot;wiki is real&quot; trope to dismiss the <a href="https://wikivisually.com/wiki/%27Pataphysics">actual concept of pataphysics</a> is a missed opportunity, but I really like what this has to say about the urge to self-insert in the first place and about escapism in general. As always, I enjoyed the thoroughness you bring to the way the Foundation does things - my favorite detail is footnote 7, how exact they are and how the Foundation personnel themselves have to walk on eggshells.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;note&nbsp;on&nbsp;pataphysics&nbsp;though</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I finally found the old page about this (linked above) that Hippo posted last year, which explains the cool parts of this better than I could.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>One attempt at a definition might be to say that 'pataphysics is a branch of philosophy or science that examines imaginary phenomena that exist in a world beyond metaphysics; it is the science of imaginary solutions.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>Non-figurative: Tom and Alice stood side by side in the lunch line.</p>
<p>Metaphor:<br />
Tom and Alice stood side by side in the lunch line, two pieces positioned on a chessboard.</p>
<p>Pataphor: Tom took a step closer to Alice and made a date for Friday night, checkmating. Rudy was furious at losing to Margaret so easily and dumped the board on the rose-colored quilt, stomping downstairs.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>We already use pataphors on the site all the time - for example, Humes are pretty much a metaphor for the level of reality in an area; having the characters build a de-hume-idifier and calling it a Reality Anchor is basically using a metaphorical solution to solve a metaphorical problem. I <em>love that shit</em>.</p>
<p>I'm put off by the circlejerkitude of &quot;omg guys our writing is really real&quot; too, but it only makes sense to me that the SCPF would in some realities have the insight to realize how much of what they're doing makes no sense in an existence that's not composed of narrative, and figure out how to turn the situation to their benefit (whether or not they're explicitly aware of why it works). I think there's a lot of potential there and wouldn't want to see the baby thrown out with the bathwater.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

